ariable unit: people\n                        Variable description:  Inhabitants. "If we use the population density range derived from Erlitou (60-100 persons/ha), as discussed earlier in text, Zhengzhou’s population would have been 78,000-130,000 with a mean of 104,000 persons." Erlitou\'s population declined in Phase IV (1600-1500 BCE). Liu (2006) uses number of pits and burials to calculate population range.<br>Zhengzhou.<br>[6,000-12,000] for area inside unknown wall. [60,000-120,000] for area inside inner wall. [300,000-600,000] for area inside outer wall. [500,000-1,000,000] for total area of site.<br>If the walled area enclosed 30 hectares (calculated from length of a perimeter wall of 6,960 meters) and its population density was between 200-400 persons per hectare (a standard based on ancient cities), the number of inhabitants at the core would be between 6,000-12,000.<br>However, the above wall is not the only one. According to Shelach and Jaffe (2014) "The inner walls of Zhengzhou enclosed an area of about 300 ha, but the area inside the recently discovered outer walls was as large as 1,500 ha (Fig. 8)." This would provide an estimate of between 60,000-120,000 for the area inside the inner wall.<br>If the city of Zhengzhou is considered to be the whole site, spread over 25km2 or 2500 hectares, the estimated number of inhabitants balloons to 500,000 at the (relatively low) density per hectare of 200 people.\n                    '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 2344, 'temperature': 0.2})
